insert into public.vocab_words (sort_order, word, fast_meaning, example_sentence)
values
  (1, 'perfunctory', 'done without real care', 'Her perfunctory apology satisfied the rule but not the students who felt ignored. | He gave a perfunctory nod to the guard. | The inspection was merely perfunctory. | She offered a perfunctory smile. | A perfunctory glance revealed nothing.'),
  (2, 'equivocal', 'open to several interpretations', 'The witness gave an equivocal answer that neither confirmed nor denied the claim. | The lab results were entirely equivocal. | He maintained an equivocal stance on the issue. | Her equivocal tone left us guessing. | Equivocal instructions lead to mistakes.'),
  (3, 'inimical', 'hostile or harmful to', 'The new policy was inimical to small publishers because it favored large platforms. | The dry climate is inimical to these plants. | Such high taxes are inimical to growth. | Their strict rules are inimical to creativity. | Chronic stress is inimical to good health.'),
  (4, 'assiduous', 'hardworking and persistent', 'Through assiduous research the student found patterns that earlier readers had missed. | He was assiduous in his daily training. | The team made assiduous efforts to repair the bridge. | She is an assiduous reader of history. | Assiduous practice leads to mastery.'),
  (5, 'cogent', 'clear and convincing', 'The essay became more cogent after the writer replaced anecdotes with evidence. | She presented a cogent argument for the budget increase. | His explanation was surprisingly cogent. | We need a more cogent strategy. | The lawyer''s cogent defense swayed the jury.'),
  (6, 'specious', 'false but seeming plausible', 'His specious excuse sounded reasonable until the dates proved it impossible. | The advertisement made specious claims about weight loss. | She recognized his specious reasoning immediately. | It was a specious argument designed to distract. | The theory proved to be entirely specious.'),
  (7, 'tenable', 'able to be defended', 'The theory remained tenable because new data supported its main prediction. | His position is no longer tenable. | We need a tenable solution to this crisis. | The fort was tenable against the siege. | She offered a tenable alternative to the plan.'),
  (8, 'obviate', 'make no longer necessary', 'Clear instructions obviate many mistakes that would otherwise delay the experiment. | The new vaccine could obviate the need for lockdowns. | A parachute obviates the danger of falling. | Automation obviates much of the manual labor. | Good planning obviates future crises.'),
  (9, 'circumspect', 'careful to avoid risk', 'The historian was circumspect when judging a source with an unknown author. | The bank is circumspect about lending money now. | He was circumspect in his responses to the press. | A circumspect approach is needed here. | She took a circumspect look around the room.'),
  (10, 'deleterious', 'harmful over time', 'The chemical had a deleterious effect on the plants after repeated exposure. | Smoking is highly deleterious to your lungs. | The policy had deleterious consequences for the economy. | Gossip can be deleterious to workplace morale. | He ignored the deleterious impact of his diet.'),
  (11, 'aberrant', 'departing from the norm', 'The aberrant result was removed only after the lab found a recording error. | Such aberrant behavior is unusual for him. | The software crashed due to an aberrant code loop. | They studied the aberrant cell growth. | The weather has been completely aberrant lately.'),
  (12, 'abate', 'become less intense', 'Public anger began to abate once officials released the missing records. | The storm will abate by morning. | We waited for the wind to abate. | His symptoms finally began to abate. | The noise from the street did not abate.'),
  (13, 'abjure', 'formally reject or renounce', 'The critic urged artists to abjure formulas and seek riskier methods. | The king was forced to abjure his faith. | She chose to abjure her former beliefs. | He abjured his allegiance to the old regime. | The cult members abjured all worldly possessions.'),
  (14, 'abstemious', 'avoiding excess or indulgence', 'Her abstemious habits made the lavish banquet seem more ceremonial than tempting. | The monk led an abstemious life. | He is abstemious with his alcohol consumption. | An abstemious diet helped her recover. | They ate an abstemious meal of bread and water.'),
  (15, 'abstruse', 'difficult to understand', 'The philosopher translated abstruse arguments into examples his audience could follow. | The physics lecture was far too abstruse. | She enjoys reading abstruse philosophical texts. | The manual was filled with abstruse terminology. | He struggled with the abstruse mathematical proof.'),
  (16, 'accede', 'agree or give in', 'The committee finally acceded to the request for a longer review period. | The company acceded to the union''s demands. | He refused to accede to their blackmail. | The prince acceded to the throne last year. | We cannot accede to these harsh terms.'),
  (17, 'acerbic', 'sharp and biting in tone', 'His acerbic review mocked the film more than it analyzed it. | The comedian is known for his acerbic wit. | She wrote an acerbic letter to the editor. | His acerbic comments hurt her feelings. | The manager''s acerbic tone created a hostile environment.'),
  (18, 'acquiesce', 'accept without protest', 'The mayor did not endorse the plan but chose to acquiesce under pressure. | She will never acquiesce to such unfair rules. | They acquiesced to the manager''s decision quietly. | He acquiesced out of sheer exhaustion. | The board acquiesced in the merger.'),
  (19, 'acumen', 'sharp practical judgment', 'Her financial acumen helped the museum survive a difficult funding year. | He lacks the business acumen to run a startup. | Her political acumen is unmatched. | The investor displayed remarkable market acumen. | It takes great acumen to solve this puzzle.'),
  (20, 'admonish', 'warn or scold firmly', 'The editor admonished the reporter for presenting rumor as fact. | The teacher admonished the students for talking. | She admonished him to be careful on the ice. | The judge admonished the lawyer''s behavior. | He was gently admonished for his tardiness.'),
  (21, 'adroit', 'skillful and clever', 'An adroit negotiator can reveal disagreement without ending the conversation. | The mechanic was adroit at fixing old engines. | She made an adroit maneuver to avoid the crash. | His adroit handling of the press saved the campaign. | The adroit thief bypassed the alarm.'),
  (22, 'adulation', 'excessive and uncritical praise', 'The scientist valued careful criticism more than the adulation of admirers. | The pop star basked in the adulation of her fans. | He sought adulation from everyone he met. | Such blind adulation can be dangerous. | The team received massive adulation after the win.'),
  (23, 'affable', 'warm and easygoing', 'The affable host made even shy visitors feel included. | The new neighbor is incredibly affable. | She has an affable personality that draws people in. | He gave an affable wave to the crowd. | The affable clerk answered all our questions.'),
  (24, 'affinity', 'natural liking or connection', 'The poet had an affinity for images drawn from ordinary city life. | He has a natural affinity for foreign languages. | She felt an immediate affinity with the stray dog. | There is a close affinity between the two theories. | His affinity for nature led him to forestry.'),
  (25, 'aggrandize', 'increase power or status', 'The ruler used public works to aggrandize himself rather than serve citizens. | The CEO sought to aggrandize his own reputation. | They used the charity to aggrandize their wealth. | His speech was an attempt to aggrandize his achievements. | The empire sought to aggrandize its borders.'),
  (26, 'alacrity', 'eager readiness to act', 'She accepted the difficult assignment with alacrity because it matched her interests. | He responded to the emergency with alacrity. | They packed their bags with alacrity for the trip. | The dog fetched the ball with joyful alacrity. | She jumped at the opportunity with alacrity.'),
  (27, 'alleviate', 'make less severe', 'The new schedule alleviated crowding without increasing the school''s budget. | The medicine helped alleviate the pain. | We must take steps to alleviate poverty. | A cold compress will alleviate the swelling. | He tried to alleviate her fears about the flight.'),
  (28, 'allay', 'reduce fear or worry', 'Transparent data helped allay concerns about the vaccine''s safety. | The principal spoke to allay the students'' anxiety. | Nothing could allay his suspicion of the stranger. | The government tried to allay fears of a recession. | A warm cup of tea helped allay her nerves.'),
  (29, 'ambivalent', 'having mixed feelings', 'He felt ambivalent about the award because it rewarded work he now questioned. | She is ambivalent about moving to a new city. | The voters are ambivalent toward the new candidate. | I remain completely ambivalent about the movie. | His ambivalent attitude frustrated his team.'),
  (30, 'ameliorate', 'make a problem better', 'The charity hoped tutoring would ameliorate the effects of lost classroom time. | The new laws ameliorate the poor working conditions. | We need a plan to ameliorate the housing crisis. | Foreign aid helped ameliorate the famine. | The manager tried to ameliorate the customer''s frustration.'),
  (31, 'amenable', 'open to persuasion', 'The council was amenable to revisions once residents proposed practical alternatives. | The children were amenable to trying the new vegetables. | He is rarely amenable to outside advice. | The schedule is completely amenable to change. | She proved amenable to the compromise.'),
  (32, 'anachronistic', 'out of its time', 'The novel''s anachronistic slang made the ancient setting feel oddly modern. | The director used anachronistic music in the period film. | His views on gender roles are completely anachronistic. | The castle features anachronistic electric lighting. | The sword in the modern setting was deliberately anachronistic.'),
  (33, 'anodyne', 'inoffensive or soothing', 'The candidate offered anodyne remarks that pleased everyone and clarified little. | The music was anodyne background noise. | She took an anodyne pill for her headache. | The corporate statement was entirely anodyne. | He offered an anodyne apology to avoid conflict.'),
  (34, 'anomalous', 'unusual or unexpected', 'The anomalous spike in sales puzzled analysts until a holiday promotion was found. | It was an anomalous warm day in the middle of winter. | The test produced anomalous results. | They investigated the anomalous radio signals. | Such anomalous behavior is cause for concern.'),
  (35, 'antagonistic', 'actively opposed or hostile', 'The two researchers were antagonistic in debate but respectful in print. | The crowd became antagonistic toward the referee. | He took an antagonistic stance during the meeting. | The rival gangs are highly antagonistic. | Her antagonistic remarks started a fight.'),
  (36, 'antipathy', 'strong feeling of dislike', 'His antipathy toward bureaucracy made the detailed application especially frustrating. | The cat showed a clear antipathy toward the new dog. | There is a historical antipathy between the two nations. | She felt a deep antipathy for dishonest people. | His antipathy to loud noises makes concerts impossible.'),
  (37, 'antithetical', 'directly opposed in nature', 'The secretive process was antithetical to the group''s stated commitment to transparency. | His luxurious lifestyle is antithetical to his charitable claims. | War is antithetical to the principles of peace. | The two philosophies are completely antithetical. | Her actions were antithetical to the company''s core values.'),
  (38, 'apathetic', 'showing little interest', 'Voters seemed apathetic until the school closure made the election feel urgent. | The students were apathetic toward the guest speaker. | He grew apathetic about his career after the demotion. | The public remains apathetic about local politics. | An apathetic workforce reduces overall productivity.'),
  (39, 'apocryphal', 'probably not authentic', 'The apocryphal story about the inventor survives because it is memorable. | The quote attributed to him is widely considered apocryphal. | She dismissed the rumor as an apocryphal tale. | History is full of apocryphal legends. | The apocryphal gospel was never included in the official text.'),
  (40, 'appease', 'calm by giving in', 'The company lowered fees to appease customers after the outage. | He bought her flowers to appease her anger. | The government tried to appease the rebels with land. | Nothing could appease the crying toddler. | They made concessions to appease the furious investors.'),
  (41, 'approbation', 'formal approval or praise', 'The proposal received the board''s approbation after months of revision. | The author sought the approbation of her peers. | The project was met with widespread approbation. | He smiled at the teacher''s nod of approbation. | The budget passed with the mayor''s explicit approbation.'),
  (42, 'arbitrary', 'based on whim', 'The deadline seemed arbitrary because no one could explain why it mattered. | The judge''s ruling felt completely arbitrary. | The rules were enforced in an arbitrary manner. | He made an arbitrary decision to paint the room green. | An arbitrary limit was placed on the number of attendees.'),
  (43, 'arcane', 'known by few', 'The manual was full of arcane terms that confused new technicians. | He studies arcane religious rituals. | The building''s architecture involves arcane geometric principles. | She possesses arcane knowledge of medieval herbs. | The arcane rules of the club are never written down.'),
  (44, 'ardent', 'deeply enthusiastic or passionate', 'An ardent defender of libraries spoke at every budget meeting. | He is an ardent supporter of the local football team. | The poet was an ardent lover of nature. | She has an ardent desire to travel the world. | His ardent speech inspired the crowd.'),
  (45, 'arduous', 'requiring great effort', 'The arduous climb rewarded hikers with a view of the entire valley. | Writing the encyclopedia was an arduous task. | They survived an arduous journey across the desert. | The recovery from the surgery will be arduous. | He faced an arduous battle in court.'),
  (46, 'artifice', 'clever trick or deception', 'The magician''s artifice depended on directing attention away from his hands. | The politician used artifice to dodge the question. | Her tears were merely an artifice to gain sympathy. | The plot of the movie relies on clever artifice. | He saw through the salesman''s cheap artifice.'),
  (47, 'ascetic', 'self denying and austere', 'The monk''s ascetic routine left little room for comfort or distraction. | The hermit lived an ascetic life in the mountains. | She adopted an ascetic diet to improve her health. | His ascetic apartment contained only a bed and a desk. | The ascetic practices included long periods of fasting.'),
  (48, 'asperity', 'harshness of tone', 'The professor''s asperity faded when the nervous student explained the mistake. | He replied with surprising asperity to the simple question. | The asperity of the winter wind was brutal. | She spoke with an asperity that shocked her friends. | The manager''s asperity alienated his employees.'),
  (49, 'assuage', 'soothe or relieve', 'A clear apology helped assuage the anger of the overlooked volunteers. | The mother tried to assuage her child''s fears of the dark. | A heavy meal will assuage your hunger. | He donated to charity to assuage his guilt. | The cool water assuaged the burning sensation.'),
  (50, 'astute', 'shrewd and perceptive', 'The astute critic noticed how the painting echoed earlier political posters. | She is an astute observer of human behavior. | His astute investments made him wealthy. | An astute manager anticipates problems before they happen. | The detective made an astute deduction based on the footprints.'),
  (51, 'attenuate', 'weaken or reduce', 'Distance attenuates sound so the bells seemed faint across the lake. | The earplugs attenuate the noise of the machinery. | The drug was designed to attenuate the virus''s effects. | The atmosphere attenuates the sun''s radiation. | Her influence began to attenuate after the scandal.'),
  (52, 'austere', 'plain and severe', 'The austere building used bare concrete rather than decorative stone. | The headmaster was an austere man who rarely smiled. | They lived an austere life on the remote farm. | The austere landscape had a harsh beauty. | The budget required austere financial measures.'),
  (53, 'avarice', 'greed for wealth', 'The fable warns that avarice can make prosperity feel like poverty. | The corrupt official was driven by sheer avarice. | His avarice led him to embezzle company funds. | Avarice destroyed their long-standing friendship. | The novel critiques the avarice of the upper class.'),
  (54, 'aver', 'state with confidence', 'The biologist averred that the pattern was real rather than accidental. | The witness averred that she had seen the defendant at the scene. | He averred his innocence until the very end. | The company avers that its products are entirely safe. | She averred that the painting was a genuine masterpiece.'),
  (55, 'aversion', 'strong feeling of dislike', 'Her aversion to shortcuts made her a careful but slow editor. | I have a deep aversion to raw fish. | His aversion to public speaking is well known. | She felt a sudden aversion to the loud music. | He overcame his aversion to flying by taking lessons.'),
  (56, 'banal', 'dull and unoriginal', 'The speech relied on banal slogans instead of specific plans. | The pop song''s lyrics were completely banal. | He made a banal comment about the weather. | The movie was full of banal plot twists. | She was bored by the banal conversation at the party.'),
  (57, 'belie', 'give a false impression', 'His calm voice belied the anxiety he felt before the interview. | The peaceful landscape belies the region''s violent history. | Her youthful energy belies her true age. | His smile belied the anger in his eyes. | The simple design belies the machine''s complex function.'),
  (58, 'belligerent', 'hostile and aggressive', 'The belligerent response turned a minor disagreement into a public feud. | The drunk customer became belligerent with the bartender. | Two belligerent nations amassed troops on the border. | His belligerent attitude made teamwork impossible. | She gave him a belligerent glare across the room.'),
  (59, 'benign', 'gentle or harmless', 'The rumor proved benign once the full context became public. | The doctor assured him the tumor was benign. | She has a benign smile that puts everyone at ease. | The climate in this region is quite benign. | The chemical is benign and safe for the environment.'),
  (60, 'bolster', 'strengthen or support', 'The archaeologist used carbon dating to bolster her interpretation of the site. | More pillows were added to bolster the injured leg. | The unexpected praise helped bolster his confidence. | They sent reinforcements to bolster the defense line. | The evidence serves to bolster the prosecutor''s case.'),
  (61, 'brazen', 'bold without shame', 'The brazen forgery included a signature copied from a museum poster. | The thieves made a brazen daytime robbery. | He told a brazen lie right to her face. | Her brazen disrespect for authority shocked the class. | The brazen request for more money was denied immediately.'),
  (62, 'brevity', 'concise use of words', 'The editor praised the essay''s brevity because every sentence served a purpose. | The brevity of the meeting was a pleasant surprise. | Poetry relies heavily on brevity and impact. | He is known for the extreme brevity of his emails. | The brevity of human life is a common theme in literature.'),
  (63, 'brusque', 'abrupt and curt', 'The clerk''s brusque reply made the routine request feel unwelcome. | The doctor was brusque but highly efficient. | A brusque dismissal ended the conversation instantly. | Her brusque manner often offended new acquaintances. | He gave a brusque nod and walked away.'),
  (64, 'burgeon', 'grow or expand rapidly', 'Interest in urban gardening began to burgeon after the city offered vacant lots. | The town''s population began to burgeon during the gold rush. | The burgeon of new technologies changed the industry. | Her confidence burgeoned as she won more matches. | The local art scene is burgeoning.'),
  (65, 'buttress', 'support or reinforce', 'The lawyer cited precedent to buttress her argument before the court. | The stone walls were supported by massive wooden buttresses. | He gathered more data to buttress his theory. | The emotional support from his friends buttressed his resolve. | The treaty was designed to buttress regional security.'),
  (66, 'cacophony', 'harsh mixture of sounds', 'The market''s cacophony made it hard to hear the guide''s instructions. | A cacophony of car horns echoed through the tunnel. | The orchestra tuning up created a terrible cacophony. | The classroom became a cacophony of shouting children. | He couldn''t think straight in the cacophony of the factory.'),
  (67, 'candid', 'honest and direct', 'The mentor''s candid feedback helped the writer improve the weak conclusion. | Let me be perfectly candid with you about your performance. | The photographer caught a candid moment of laughter. | She gave a candid interview about her struggles. | His candid admission of guilt was refreshing.'),
  (68, 'capricious', 'changing in unpredictable ways', 'The capricious weather forced organizers to move the concert indoors twice. | The capricious king changed his mind daily. | Her capricious moods made her hard to work with. | The stock market is famously capricious. | It was a capricious decision based on no logic.'),
  (69, 'castigate', 'criticize in a severe way', 'Reviewers castigated the report for ignoring evidence that contradicted its claims. | The principal castigated the students for their vandalism. | She castigated herself for making such a foolish error. | The media castigated the politician for the scandal. | He was castigated by his coach for missing practice.'),
  (70, 'catalyze', 'cause action or change', 'The discovery catalyzed a new debate about how the species evolved. | The enzyme helps catalyze the chemical reaction. | The protest catalyzed a nationwide movement for reform. | The new CEO catalyzed a massive restructuring. | The tragic event catalyzed her desire to help others.'),
  (71, 'caustic', 'harshly critical or biting', 'Her caustic comments damaged morale even when her points were accurate. | The caustic chemical burned a hole in the fabric. | He is known for his caustic sense of humor. | The critic wrote a caustic review of the play. | She delivered a caustic reply that silenced the room.'),
  (72, 'censure', 'officially criticize or condemn', 'The board voted to censure the official for concealing the audit. | The senator faced public censure for his remarks. | The newspaper''s editorial was a severe censure of the mayor. | The medical council censured the doctor''s unethical practices. | She narrowly avoided formal censure by apologizing.'),
  (73, 'chicanery', 'trickery or deception', 'The contract''s confusing language seemed designed for chicanery rather than clarity. | The accountant was fired for financial chicanery. | Political chicanery ruined the election process. | He resorted to chicanery to win the game. | The plot involved layers of corporate chicanery.'),
  (74, 'circumscribe', 'limit or restrict', 'Budget rules circumscribed the director''s ability to hire additional staff. | The fence circumscribes the perimeter of the property. | His influence is strictly circumscribed by the new laws. | A circle can cleanly circumscribe the triangle. | Our options are circumscribed by a lack of time.'),
  (75, 'clamorous', 'loud and demanding', 'A clamorous crowd gathered outside the station after the trains stopped. | The clamorous demands of the protesters echoed in the square. | A flock of clamorous birds woke me at dawn. | The kitchen was a clamorous environment during the dinner rush. | He tried to silence the clamorous children.'),
  (76, 'clandestine', 'secret and concealed', 'The journalists uncovered a clandestine effort to influence the study. | They held clandestine meetings in the basement. | The spy ran a clandestine operation across the border. | Their clandestine romance was finally discovered. | The group planned a clandestine escape.'),
  (77, 'coalesce', 'come together into one', 'Several small protests coalesced into a national movement for reform. | The puddles coalesced into a large stream. | The various ideas finally coalesced into a single plan. | The factions coalesced to fight a common enemy. | The stars coalesced from clouds of gas and dust.'),
  (78, 'coherent', 'logical and consistent', 'The scattered notes became a coherent argument after careful organization. | The patient was barely coherent after the accident. | The team lacked a coherent strategy. | She explained the complex theory in a coherent way. | The novel''s plot is tight and coherent.'),
  (79, 'commensurate', 'equal in measure', 'The punishment seemed commensurate with the offense rather than excessive. | Her salary is commensurate with her extensive experience. | The size of the reward was commensurate to the risk taken. | They requested a budget commensurate with their needs. | His response was completely commensurate with the threat.'),
  (80, 'complacent', 'self satisfied and unaware', 'Early success made the team complacent and less attentive to errors. | We cannot afford to become complacent about security. | The complacent student failed the final exam. | The company lost its edge due to complacent management. | His complacent smile annoyed his hard-working peers.'),
  (81, 'conciliatory', 'intended to reduce conflict', 'The manager adopted a conciliatory tone after the meeting grew tense. | She offered a conciliatory gesture to end the argument. | The diplomat''s conciliatory approach prevented a war. | He wrote a conciliatory letter of apology. | The union leaders accepted the conciliatory offer.'),
  (82, 'concomitant', 'naturally happening along with', 'Greater freedom brought a concomitant need for stronger personal responsibility. | The drug''s concomitant side effects include nausea. | The rise in pollution is a concomitant of industrial growth. | Concomitant symptoms make the disease hard to diagnose. | The concomitant rain and wind caused severe damage.'),
  (83, 'conjectural', 'based on guesswork', 'The article''s conclusion remained conjectural because the key records were missing. | The theory is purely conjectural at this stage. | Her timeline of events is largely conjectural. | Conjectural evidence is not enough for a conviction. | The alien life hypothesis is highly conjectural.'),
  (84, 'construe', 'interpret in a way', 'Readers may construe the ending as hopeful rather than tragic. | How do you construe the meaning of this ancient text? | His silence was construed as agreement. | I construed her remark as a compliment. | The law can be construed in several different ways.'),
  (85, 'contentious', 'likely to cause disagreement', 'The contentious proposal divided residents who otherwise shared similar goals. | The divorce proceedings were highly contentious. | Gun control is a deeply contentious issue. | The board had a contentious debate over the budget. | He has a contentious personality and loves to argue.'),
  (86, 'contrite', 'genuinely sorry for wrongdoing', 'The contrite student revised the article and acknowledged the error publicly. | He wrote a contrite apology letter to his friend. | The thief seemed contrite before the judge. | A contrite heart is the first step to forgiveness. | She offered a genuinely contrite expression.'),
  (87, 'contravene', 'violate or oppose', 'The policy would contravene the law if applied to private speech. | The company''s actions contravene international trade agreements. | Do not contravene the rules of the competition. | The new evidence contravenes the initial report. | To contravene this order will result in immediate termination.'),
  (88, 'conundrum', 'difficult puzzle or problem', 'The missing data created a conundrum that no simple model could solve. | The ethical conundrum kept the philosopher awake at night. | Finding a cure for the disease is a medical conundrum. | He faced the conundrum of choosing between two great jobs. | The locked room presented a classic mystery conundrum.'),
  (89, 'convoluted', 'complicated in unnecessary ways', 'The instructions were so convoluted that experienced volunteers made mistakes. | The movie''s plot was excessively convoluted. | The lawyer''s convoluted argument confused the jury. | We got lost in the convoluted maze of city streets. | The tax code is famously convoluted.'),
  (90, 'corroborate', 'confirm with evidence', 'Two photographs corroborated the witness''s account of the event. | The DNA evidence corroborates the suspect''s alibi. | Recent studies corroborate the earlier findings. | He needed someone to corroborate his story. | The ancient texts corroborate the existence of the lost city.'),
  (91, 'coterie', 'small exclusive group', 'A coterie of critics shaped the magazine''s tastes for decades. | The president is surrounded by a loyal coterie of advisors. | A small coterie of artists dominated the gallery scene. | The exclusive club was run by a wealthy coterie. | She belonged to a fashionable coterie in Paris.'),
  (92, 'copious', 'abundant and plentiful', 'The archive contained copious letters from the poet''s later years. | She took copious notes during the long lecture. | The feast included copious amounts of food and wine. | He suffered copious bleeding from the wound. | The region receives copious rainfall in the spring.'),
  (93, 'credulous', 'too ready to believe', 'A credulous audience accepted the hoax before experts examined it. | The credulous investor lost all his money in the scam. | Children are naturally credulous. | The cult leader exploited his credulous followers. | A credulous public easily falls for sensational news.'),
  (94, 'cursory', 'quick and superficial', 'A cursory reading missed the irony that shaped the passage. | The guard gave the ID badge a cursory glance. | The mechanic did a cursory inspection of the engine. | A cursory search of the room turned up nothing. | She offered a cursory apology before rushing off.'),
  (95, 'cynical', 'distrustful of motives', 'The columnist''s cynical tone suggested every reformer sought only power. | He is cynical about politicians and their promises. | Her cynical worldview makes it hard for her to trust people. | The cynical marketing campaign exploited people''s fears. | A cynical laugh escaped him.'),
  (96, 'daunting', 'intimidating because of difficulty', 'The project seemed daunting until the class divided it into smaller tasks. | Climbing Everest is a truly daunting challenge. | She faced the daunting task of cleaning the entire house. | The daunting list of requirements discouraged many applicants. | The financial cost of the reform is daunting.'),
  (97, 'dearth', 'lack or scarcity', 'A dearth of reliable records made the early history difficult to reconstruct. | There is a glaring dearth of women in leadership roles here. | The region suffers from a dearth of clean drinking water. | The dearth of evidence led to an acquittal. | We face a dearth of creative ideas.'),
  (98, 'decorous', 'proper and restrained', 'The ceremony remained decorous despite strong disagreement among the guests. | The Victorian era was known for its decorous behavior. | She maintained a decorous silence during the outburst. | The party was a highly decorous affair. | His decorous manner impressed the strict grandmother.'),
  (99, 'deference', 'respectful yielding to others', 'The intern listened with deference when the veteran engineer explained the flaw. | They bowed in deference to the visiting king. | Out of deference to her age we gave her the best seat. | He treats his mentors with immense deference. | The decision was made in deference to public opinion.'),
  (100, 'deft', 'skillful and quick', 'The novelist made a deft transition from humor to grief. | The surgeon''s deft hands completed the procedure flawlessly. | She made a deft maneuver to avoid the pothole. | The politician''s deft handling of the crisis saved his career. | A deft touch is required to paint the miniature.'),
  (101, 'delineate', 'describe or outline clearly', 'The map delineated the disputed border more precisely than earlier surveys. | The contract clearly delineates the responsibilities of each party. | He delineated his plan for the company''s future. | The law delineates exactly what constitutes fraud. | The boundary was delineated by a row of trees.'),
  (102, 'denigrate', 'criticize unfairly or harshly', 'The review denigrated popular fiction without examining its artistic methods. | Bullies often denigrate their victims to feel powerful. | The candidate denigrated his opponent''s record in office. | You shouldn''t denigrate her hard work just because you disagree. | The newspaper denigrated the movement as a fad.'),
  (103, 'deprecate', 'express disapproval of', 'The author deprecated the myth that genius requires isolation. | He is fiercely self-deprecating when discussing his own success. | The software update deprecated several older features. | She deprecated the violent protests. | The professor deprecated the use of unverified sources.'),
  (104, 'derivative', 'lacking in original qualities', 'Critics found the sequel derivative because it repeated the first film''s twists. | The band''s new album feels highly derivative of earlier rock music. | The artwork is derivative and lacks imagination. | His thesis is just a derivative summary of existing research. | The recipe is merely a derivative of a classic French dish.'),
  (105, 'despondent', 'deeply discouraged or hopeless', 'The team grew despondent after repeated failures but continued testing the design. | He became despondent when he lost his job. | The despondent dog waited by the door for its owner. | A despondent mood settled over the ruined city. | She felt utterly despondent after the breakup.'),
  (106, 'desultory', 'lacking clear plan', 'Their desultory discussion wandered from funding to furniture without solving anything. | He made a desultory attempt to clean his room. | The students had a desultory conversation during the break. | The investigation was conducted in a desultory manner. | She gave a desultory wave as the train departed.'),
  (107, 'deter', 'discourage from acting', 'Higher fines may deter companies from dumping waste illegally. | The heavy rain did not deter the marathon runners. | The security cameras are meant to deter theft. | Nothing will deter him from achieving his goal. | The harsh criticism failed to deter her.'),
  (108, 'detriment', 'source of harm', 'The shortcut saved time but worked to the detriment of accuracy. | He worked long hours to the detriment of his health. | The new tax will be a detriment to small businesses. | The rumors caused serious detriment to her reputation. | Excessive screen time can be a detriment to sleep.'),
  (109, 'didactic', 'intended to teach', 'The novel is didactic but never forgets to tell an engaging story. | The children''s show is both entertaining and didactic. | His tone was overly didactic and patronizing. | The museum exhibit has a clear didactic purpose. | The fable ends with a didactic moral.'),
  (110, 'diffident', 'shy and lacking confidence', 'The diffident speaker became more assured after the audience responded warmly. | The diffident child hid behind his mother''s legs. | She gave a diffident smile when praised. | He was too diffident to ask for a raise. | A diffident knock at the door announced the visitor.'),
  (111, 'dilatory', 'tending to cause delay', 'The senator''s dilatory tactics postponed the vote for another week. | The contractor''s dilatory work caused the project to run late. | Stop making dilatory excuses and start working. | The legal team used dilatory motions to stall the trial. | Her dilatory response frustrated the client.'),
  (112, 'diminution', 'decrease or reduction', 'The river''s diminution alarmed farmers who depended on it for irrigation. | There has been a steady diminution of the local wildlife. | The treaty led to a diminution of nuclear weapons. | A diminution in sales caused the store to close. | Aging often brings a diminution of physical strength.'),
  (113, 'disabuse', 'free from false belief', 'The coach tried to disabuse players of the idea that talent replaced practice. | Let me disabuse you of the notion that this will be easy. | The article disabuses readers of common medical myths. | He quickly disabused her of the romanticized view of war. | The failure disabused him of his arrogance.'),
  (114, 'discerning', 'showing good judgment', 'A discerning reader notices how the narrator avoids difficult questions. | The discerning customer spotted the flaw in the fabric. | He is a discerning judge of fine wine. | A discerning eye is required to appreciate this art. | The hiring manager made a highly discerning choice.'),
  (115, 'discordant', 'conflicting or harsh', 'The cheerful music felt discordant with the scene of abandoned streets. | The orchestra sounded discordant during the first rehearsal. | Her loud laughter was discordant in the quiet library. | The meeting ended with discordant views on the budget. | The two witness accounts were completely discordant.'),
  (116, 'discredit', 'damage reputation or trust', 'The fabricated quotation threatened to discredit the entire article. | The scandal completely discredited the politician. | The lawyer tried to discredit the witness''s testimony. | The new evidence discredited the old theory. | He sought to discredit his rival''s scientific findings.'),
  (117, 'disparate', 'different in essential ways', 'The study linked disparate fields such as economics and ecology. | The group was made of people from widely disparate backgrounds. | It is hard to unite such disparate ideas into one cohesive plan. | The city is a collection of disparate neighborhoods. | The museum holds a disparate array of artifacts.'),
  (118, 'dispassionate', 'calm and impartial', 'A dispassionate analysis revealed strengths that critics had ignored. | The judge delivered a perfectly dispassionate verdict. | As a journalist she must maintain a dispassionate view. | The doctor gave a dispassionate report of the injuries. | The scientific method requires dispassionate observation.'),
  (119, 'dissemble', 'hide true motives', 'The diplomat tried to dissemble her concern but her silence was revealing. | He smiled to dissemble his real intentions. | The spy was trained to dissemble under pressure. | She proved unable to dissemble her deep disappointment. | Politicians often dissemble to avoid hard questions.'),
  (120, 'dissident', 'opposing official views', 'Dissident writers circulated essays that challenged the regime''s version of history. | The dissident group protested outside the embassy. | The government arrested the outspoken dissident. | Dissident voices were quickly silenced by the dictator. | The church expelled the dissident members.'),
  (121, 'doctrinaire', 'rigidly guided by ideology', 'His doctrinaire approach left no room for evidence that complicated the theory. | The doctrinaire communist refused to accept market reforms. | Her views are too doctrinaire for mainstream politics. | The party became increasingly doctrinaire over time. | He gave a doctrinaire speech that alienated moderates.'),
  (122, 'dogmatic', 'asserting opinions rigidly', 'The dogmatic lecturer treated every question as a challenge to authority. | The cult leader was incredibly dogmatic in his teachings. | She has a dogmatic insistence on doing things her way. | A dogmatic approach stifles genuine scientific inquiry. | He is dogmatic about the benefits of his strict diet.'),
  (123, 'dubious', 'doubtful or suspect', 'The committee rejected the study because its methods were dubious. | The salesman made a dubious claim about the car''s history. | I am extremely dubious about his promised return on investment. | The painting''s origins remain highly dubious. | She gave him a dubious look when he offered to help.'),
  (124, 'ebullient', 'cheerful and energetic', 'Her ebullient speech lifted the mood after a difficult debate. | The ebullient child bounced around the playground. | The team was in an ebullient mood after the championship win. | His ebullient personality makes him a great host. | She gave an ebullient laugh that filled the room.'),
  (125, 'eclectic', 'drawn from diverse sources', 'The museum''s eclectic collection included textiles as well as scientific tools and sculpture. | Her musical tastes are extremely eclectic. | The house was decorated with an eclectic mix of furniture. | The chef created an eclectic menu blending several cuisines. | He possesses an eclectic library of rare books.'),
  (126, 'edifying', 'morally or intellectually useful', 'The documentary was edifying because it explained both the science and the stakes. | The lecture on classical architecture was highly edifying. | I found his perspective on the matter to be quite edifying. | The fable provides an edifying lesson for children. | The religious retreat proved to be a deeply edifying experience.'),
  (127, 'efface', 'erase or make obscure', 'Time began to efface the inscription on the old monument. | He tried to efface the memory of the terrible accident. | The dictator sought to efface his rival''s name from history. | She acts as a self-effacing assistant despite her talent. | The waves effaced the footprints in the sand.'),
  (128, 'effusive', 'showing excessive enthusiasm', 'The coach was effusive in praising the backup goalkeeper''s performance. | She wrote an effusive thank-you letter to her host. | The critic''s review was incredibly effusive. | He offered effusive apologies for his mistake. | The fans gave the band an effusive welcome.'),
  (129, 'egalitarian', 'favoring equal rights', 'The club adopted egalitarian rules that gave every member one vote. | The Scandinavian countries are known for their egalitarian societies. | The school promotes an egalitarian learning environment. | The constitution is built on egalitarian principles. | They share household chores in a strictly egalitarian manner.'),
  (130, 'egregious', 'shockingly bad or obvious', 'The article contained an egregious error in its opening paragraph. | The company committed an egregious violation of safety standards. | His egregious behavior at the wedding ruined the event. | It was an egregious breach of trust. | The foul on the field was incredibly egregious.'),
  (131, 'elicit', 'draw out a response', 'The photograph elicited memories that the interview questions had missed. | The comedian failed to elicit any laughter from the audience. | The survey is designed to elicit honest opinions. | The police used harsh tactics to elicit a confession. | Her sad story elicited tears from the listeners.'),
  (132, 'elucidate', 'make clear or understandable', 'The diagram helped elucidate a process the text described poorly. | The professor tried to elucidate the complex theorem. | Could you elucidate that concept further? | The footnotes serve to elucidate the poem''s historical references. | The investigation will hopefully elucidate the cause of the fire.'),
  (133, 'empirical', 'based on observation', 'The hypothesis gained strength when empirical data supported its prediction. | The study relies solely on empirical evidence. | We need empirical proof before changing the policy. | The empirical results contradict the mathematical model. | The scientist favored empirical methods over pure theory.'),
  (134, 'encumber', 'burden or hinder', 'Excessive paperwork can encumber teachers who need time for students. | The hiker was encumbered by a heavy backpack. | The estate is encumbered with massive debts. | Do not encumber the project with unnecessary features. | The strict regulations encumber small businesses.'),
  (135, 'endemic', 'regularly found in place', 'Corruption had become endemic in the agency before the reforms began. | Malaria is endemic in many tropical regions. | This species of bird is endemic to the island. | Apathy seems endemic among the current voter base. | The endemic disease wiped out the crop.'),
  (136, 'enervate', 'weaken or drain energy', 'The long delay seemed to enervate even the project''s strongest supporters. | The intense heat enervated the marathon runners. | The dull routine enervates my creative spirit. | An enervating disease left him bedridden. | The constant arguments enervated the entire household.'),
  (137, 'engender', 'produce or cause', 'The court''s decision engendered debate about privacy and public safety. | Good leadership engenders trust among employees. | The harsh policy engendered deep resentment. | The tragedy engendered a sense of unity in the community. | The new technology engendered fear of job losses.'),
  (138, 'enigmatic', 'mysterious and hard to interpret', 'The artist''s enigmatic smile has invited centuries of speculation. | The enigmatic message left the detectives puzzled. | The movie ends on a highly enigmatic note. | He is an enigmatic figure in local politics. | The ruins offer only enigmatic clues to the past.'),
  (139, 'ephemeral', 'lasting a short time', 'The app''s popularity was ephemeral and faded within months. | The beauty of a cherry blossom is famous for being ephemeral. | Fame is an ephemeral concept in the digital age. | The ephemeral joy of winning soon faded into exhaustion. | They chased the ephemeral lights of the aurora.'),
  (140, 'equanimity', 'calm under stress', 'The pilot handled the emergency with remarkable equanimity. | She accepted the devastating news with surprising equanimity. | The monk maintained his equanimity despite the chaos around him. | It is hard to keep your equanimity during a heated argument. | His equanimity in the face of danger is legendary.'),
  (141, 'equivocate', 'avoid a clear answer', 'When asked about costs the official chose to equivocate. | The politician equivocated when asked about his past votes. | Stop equivocating and give me a straight answer. | The suspect equivocated during the police interrogation. | The lawyer accused the witness of equivocating on the stand.'),
  (142, 'erratic', 'irregular or unpredictable', 'Erratic attendance made it difficult to measure the program''s impact. | The patient''s heartbeat was erratic and weak. | His erratic driving caught the attention of the police. | The stock market has been highly erratic this week. | The bird''s erratic flight path suggested an injured wing.'),
  (143, 'erudite', 'showing deep learning', 'The erudite essay connected a modern play to ancient myths. | The professor is an erudite scholar of ancient languages. | She gave an erudite lecture on medieval history. | The erudite book won several academic awards. | His erudite vocabulary often confuses his friends.'),
  (144, 'esoteric', 'understood by few', 'The article made esoteric legal concepts accessible to ordinary readers. | The ritual involved esoteric symbols and chants. | He collects books on esoteric philosophical topics. | The programming language is too esoteric for beginners. | The joke relied on an esoteric pop culture reference.'),
  (145, 'estimable', 'worthy of respect', 'Her estimable commitment to accuracy earned the trust of colleagues. | The foundation made an estimable contribution to the arts. | He is an estimable opponent on the debate stage. | The judge has an estimable record of fairness. | Her estimable efforts saved the historic building.'),
  (146, 'evasive', 'avoiding direct answers', 'The witness sounded evasive when asked about the missing funds. | The military aircraft took evasive action to dodge the missile. | He gave an evasive reply regarding his whereabouts last night. | Her evasive behavior made everyone suspicious. | The evasive animal disappeared into the dense brush.'),
  (147, 'exacting', 'requiring great care', 'Restoring the fragile manuscript was exacting work. | The exacting chef demanded perfection from his kitchen staff. | Writing computer code is an extremely exacting process. | The exacting standards of the factory ensured high quality. | She is an exacting teacher who accepts no excuses.'),
  (148, 'exacerbate', 'make a problem worse', 'Cutting the bus routes would exacerbate traffic near the school. | Scratching the rash will only exacerbate the itch. | The new tax law exacerbated the economic crisis. | His angry response exacerbated the delicate situation. | The wind exacerbated the spread of the wildfire.'),
  (149, 'exculpate', 'clear from blame', 'New evidence helped exculpate the researcher accused of misconduct. | The security footage completely exculpated the prime suspect. | The grand jury refused to exculpate the officer. | The report exculpates the management of any negligence. | He desperately sought evidence to exculpate himself.'),
  (150, 'exigent', 'requiring immediate action', 'The flood created exigent needs that delayed long term planning. | Exigent circumstances required the police to enter without a warrant. | The exigent timeline forced the team to work overnight. | The hospital faced exigent demands during the pandemic. | The manager handled the exigent problem immediately.'),
  (151, 'exonerate', 'prove not guilty', 'The DNA results helped exonerate a man wrongly convicted years earlier. | The internal investigation exonerated the mayor. | The dashcam video will exonerate the driver. | The committee voted to exonerate the accused student. | He felt immense relief when the court exonerated him.'),
  (152, 'expedient', 'convenient but possibly improper', 'The shortcut was expedient but it undermined the study''s credibility. | It was politically expedient to agree with the popular opinion. | The general chose the most expedient route despite the risks. | The expedient solution is not always the ethical one. | They used an expedient lie to avoid trouble.'),
  (153, 'extrapolate', 'infer beyond known data', 'Scientists extrapolate future trends from measurements collected over many decades. | It is risky to extrapolate global conclusions from one local study. | Economists extrapolate growth patterns based on current spending. | We can extrapolate the animal''s size from its footprints. | Do not extrapolate his future behavior from one mistake.'),
  (154, 'facetious', 'joking at an inappropriate time', 'His facetious remark annoyed colleagues who were discussing a serious risk. | Please stop being facetious during the safety briefing. | She offered a facetious apology that only angered him more. | The tone of the article was entirely facetious. | The judge did not appreciate the lawyer''s facetious comments.'),
  (155, 'fallacious', 'based on faulty reasoning', 'The argument was fallacious because it confused correlation with causation. | The advertisement makes a fallacious claim about the product''s benefits. | His logic is fundamentally fallacious. | The article is built on fallacious assumptions. | Don''t be fooled by her fallacious statistics.'),
  (156, 'fastidious', 'very attentive to detail', 'The fastidious editor noticed inconsistencies in dates and spellings. | He is famously fastidious about keeping his car clean. | The fastidious cat spent hours grooming itself. | The fastidious accountant caught the minor discrepancy. | Her fastidious preparation ensured the event was flawless.'),
  (157, 'felicitous', 'apt and well chosen', 'Her felicitous phrase captured the novel''s central tension. | The weather was incredibly felicitous for the outdoor wedding. | He has a talent for finding the most felicitous words. | The felicitous outcome of the negotiation pleased everyone. | It was a highly felicitous arrangement for both companies.'),
  (158, 'fervent', 'intensely passionate or sincere', 'Fervent supporters waited for hours to hear the reformer''s speech. | She has a fervent belief in the power of education. | The fervent prayers of the congregation filled the church. | The rebel leader delivered a fervent plea for freedom. | His fervent dedication to the sport earned him a medal.'),
  (159, 'florid', 'overly elaborate or ornate', 'The florid description buried a simple idea under decorative language. | The Victorian mansion is known for its florid architecture. | His florid complexion suggested he had been in the sun too long. | The composer''s florid style is not to everyone''s taste. | She gave a florid speech filled with grand metaphors.'),
  (160, 'foible', 'minor character flaw', 'The biography treats the inventor''s impatience as a foible rather than a vice. | His habit of constantly checking his watch is a minor foible. | We must learn to accept the foibles of our friends. | The sitcom highlights the amusing foibles of everyday life. | Her obsession with grammar is a well-known foible.'),
  (161, 'foment', 'stir up trouble', 'The pamphlet was designed to foment resentment among already anxious workers. | The dictator accused the journalists of trying to foment rebellion. | Foreign agents tried to foment a civil war. | His angry rhetoric fomented a riot in the capital. | The unfair policy fomented discontent in the office.'),
  (162, 'fortuitous', 'happening by chance', 'A fortuitous meeting at the library led to their collaboration. | The storm''s delay was a fortuitous break for the unprepared sailors. | The company made a fortuitous discovery during the routine testing. | It was purely fortuitous that I found my lost keys. | The fortuitous timing of the investment made him rich.'),
  (163, 'fractious', 'irritable and hard to manage', 'The fractious committee struggled to agree on even minor revisions. | The exhausted children became fractious during the long drive. | The fractious political climate prevents any real progress. | The horses grew fractious as the thunderstorm approached. | A fractious debate delayed the passage of the bill.'),
  (164, 'frugal', 'careful with money', 'The frugal director reused sets rather than exceed the theater''s budget. | The frugal shopper clipped coupons for hours. | They lived a frugal lifestyle to save for a house. | The frugal meal consisted of beans and rice. | Being frugal is not the same as being cheap.'),
  (165, 'furtive', 'secretive or stealthy', 'His furtive glance at the door made the guard suspicious. | The spy made a furtive exchange in the crowded park. | The dog gave a furtive sniff at the forbidden cake. | They exchanged furtive whispers during the exam. | A furtive figure slipped into the alleyway.'),
  (166, 'galvanize', 'spur into action', 'The fire galvanized residents to demand stronger safety rules. | The shocking documentary galvanized public opinion on climate change. | The coach''s halftime speech galvanized the losing team. | The tragedy galvanized the nation into passing stricter laws. | The successful protest galvanized the labor movement.'),
  (167, 'garrulous', 'talking too much', 'The garrulous guide turned a short tour into a two hour lecture. | The garrulous passenger next to me talked for the entire flight. | My garrulous neighbor often keeps me trapped on the porch. | The garrulous old man told stories of his youth. | A garrulous nature can be a liability in a secret meeting.'),
  (168, 'glib', 'smooth but shallow', 'The answer was glib and ignored the problem''s real complexity. | The glib salesman sold them a defective car. | Her glib apology failed to convince anyone of her sincerity. | The politician is known for his glib debate style. | The glib remark was considered highly insensitive.'),
  (169, 'gratuitous', 'uncalled for or unnecessary', 'The novel''s violence felt gratuitous because it added nothing to the plot. | The film was criticized for its gratuitous nudity. | The villain''s cruelty seemed entirely gratuitous. | I didn''t appreciate the gratuitous insult. | The expensive decorations felt gratuitous given the tight budget.'),
  (170, 'hackneyed', 'overused and stale', 'The essay opened with a hackneyed quote instead of a fresh claim. | The romantic comedy relied on hackneyed tropes. | His motivational speech was full of hackneyed phrases. | The plot of the action movie is completely hackneyed. | The author breathed new life into a hackneyed genre.'),
  (171, 'halting', 'hesitant and uneven', 'Her halting explanation suggested she had not fully understood the procedure. | The toddler took halting steps across the living room. | The old engine started with a halting sputter. | His halting English made communication difficult. | The peace talks moved forward at a halting pace.'),
  (172, 'haughty', 'acting arrogantly superior', 'The haughty critic dismissed the young artist before seeing the exhibit. | The haughty waiter sneered at our clothing. | She gave a haughty toss of her hair and walked away. | The haughty nobles ignored the suffering of the peasants. | His haughty demeanor alienates his coworkers.'),
  (173, 'heuristic', 'practical problem solving shortcut', 'The rule was a useful heuristic rather than a perfect law. | The programmer designed a heuristic algorithm to sort the massive data. | Using a heuristic approach saves time on complex puzzles. | The heuristic method is not guaranteed to find the absolute best answer. | Teachers use heuristic techniques to encourage independent thinking.'),
  (174, 'hubris', 'excessive and dangerous pride', 'The leader''s hubris made him ignore warnings from experienced advisers. | The tragedy of the play is caused by the hero''s hubris. | The company''s hubris led to its eventual bankruptcy. | His hubris convinced him he could never fail. | The myth of Icarus is a classic tale of deadly hubris.'),
  (175, 'iconoclastic', 'attacking established beliefs', 'Her iconoclastic essay challenged assumptions that the field had long accepted. | The iconoclastic filmmaker refuses to follow traditional Hollywood formats. | His iconoclastic views make him unpopular in the conservative town. | The iconoclastic movement sought to destroy old religious statues. | An iconoclastic approach is often needed for true innovation.'),
  (176, 'ignominious', 'shameful and humiliating', 'The champion suffered an ignominious defeat in the opening round. | The general faced an ignominious retreat after his failed strategy. | The scandal marked an ignominious end to his political career. | The team''s ignominious collapse was hard to watch. | The CEO was fired in an ignominious public disgrace.'),
  (177, 'immutable', 'not able to change', 'The law was treated as immutable until public pressure forced reform. | The laws of physics are generally considered immutable. | Her immutable faith guided her through the crisis. | The mountain range stood as an immutable feature of the landscape. | Death and taxes are often called immutable facts of life.'),
  (178, 'impassive', 'showing no emotion', 'The judge remained impassive while the crowd reacted angrily. | The soldier''s face was completely impassive during the interrogation. | The impassive statues guarded the entrance to the tomb. | She watched the dramatic scene with an impassive expression. | An impassive demeanor is useful when playing poker.'),
  (179, 'impeccable', 'without any noticeable flaws', 'Her impeccable citations made the paper easy to verify. | The butler''s manners were always impeccable. | The violin was restored to an impeccable condition. | He spoke French with an impeccable accent. | The logic of her argument is impeccable.'),
  (180, 'imperious', 'domineering and arrogant', 'The director''s imperious commands discouraged assistants from offering ideas. | The imperious CEO expects total obedience from his staff. | She waved her hand with an imperious gesture of dismissal. | His imperious attitude resents any form of questioning. | The imperious tone of the memo angered the employees.'),
  (181, 'implacable', 'impossible to appease', 'The critic remained implacable even after the author revised the chapter. | The enemy forces were implacable and refused to surrender. | An implacable hatred burned between the two rival families. | The implacable march of time affects us all. | The disease proved to be an implacable foe.'),
  (182, 'implicit', 'suggested not stated', 'The article''s implicit assumption was that economic growth benefits everyone. | We had an implicit agreement not to mention the past. | There is an implicit threat in his casual warning. | The implicit bias in the hiring process must be addressed. | Her implicit trust in him proved to be a mistake.'),
  (183, 'inadvertent', 'done without intending it', 'An inadvertent omission in the chart changed the report''s meaning. | The inadvertent deletion of the file caused a panic. | He offered an inadvertent insult by forgetting her name. | The discovery of the new chemical was entirely inadvertent. | The loud noise caused an inadvertent flinch.'),
  (184, 'incisive', 'sharply clear and insightful', 'The student''s incisive question exposed a weakness in the theory. | The journalist is known for her incisive political commentary. | The incisive review highlighted exactly why the movie failed. | An incisive mind is required for advanced mathematics. | His incisive wit can cut through any argument.'),
  (185, 'incongruous', 'strangely out of place', 'The cheerful mural seemed incongruous in the abandoned factory. | The modern glass tower looks incongruous next to the ancient cathedral. | His elegant suit was incongruous at the messy barbecue. | The incongruous blend of flavors surprisingly worked well. | Her loud laughter was incongruous in the somber room.'),
  (186, 'indefatigable', 'tireless and persistent', 'The indefatigable organizer visited every neighborhood before the vote. | The indefatigable runner finished the ultramarathon with a smile. | Her indefatigable spirit inspired the whole team. | The indefatigable search dogs finally found the survivor. | He is an indefatigable champion for human rights.'),
  (187, 'indifferent', 'having no strong preference', 'The committee was indifferent between the two dates because attendance was similar. | The universe is entirely indifferent to human suffering. | The cat seemed indifferent to the new toy. | He gave an indifferent shrug when asked what to eat. | The indifferent service at the restaurant ruined the evening.'),
  (188, 'indolent', 'lazy or inactive', 'The myth portrays the grasshopper as indolent rather than merely unlucky. | The indolent employee spent hours browsing the internet. | The heavy heat made everyone feel indolent. | The indolent cat slept in the sun all day. | His indolent nature prevented him from finding a job.'),
  (189, 'ineffable', 'too great for words', 'The view inspired an ineffable sense of calm. | The ineffable beauty of the sunset silenced the crowd. | She felt an ineffable joy when holding her newborn. | The mystical experience was described as ineffable. | The music possessed an ineffable quality of sadness.'),
  (190, 'inexorable', 'impossible to stop', 'The inexorable rise of the tide forced the campers inland. | The inexorable march of progress has changed the city forever. | The rebel army faced the inexorable advance of the empire''s forces. | The aging process is an inexorable reality. | The inexorable logic of his argument left no room for debate.'),
  (191, 'ingenuous', 'innocent and sincere', 'Her ingenuous questions revealed curiosity rather than ignorance. | The ingenuous child believed every word the magician said. | An ingenuous smile lit up her face. | The ingenuous confession shocked the cynical detectives. | It is rare to find such an ingenuous person in politics.'),
  (192, 'innocuous', 'not harmful or offensive', 'The comment seemed innocuous until it was quoted out of context. | The mushroom looks dangerous but is actually perfectly innocuous. | The bug bite proved to be an innocuous irritation. | He asked an innocuous question about the weather. | The virus strain is completely innocuous to humans.'),
  (193, 'inscrutable', 'hard to understand', 'The CEO''s inscrutable smile gave investors no hint of her plans. | The ancient language remains largely inscrutable. | The cat''s inscrutable gaze made him uncomfortable. | The motives behind the crime are completely inscrutable. | The inscrutable depths of the ocean hide many secrets.'),
  (194, 'insipid', 'dull and lacking flavor', 'The review called the play insipid because its characters never changed. | The hospital food was pale and insipid. | The insipid pop song was forgotten within a week. | She grew tired of his insipid conversational topics. | The artist rejected the insipid design in favor of something bold.'),
  (195, 'insular', 'narrow or isolated', 'The town''s insular habits made newcomers feel like permanent outsiders. | The insular community rarely interacts with the modern world. | The island''s insular ecosystem is highly vulnerable to invasive species. | The committee''s insular thinking prevented them from seeing the flaw. | The royal family lives an insular life behind palace walls.'),
  (196, 'intractable', 'hard to control or solve', 'The dispute became intractable after both sides rejected compromise. | The intractable child refused to follow any rules. | The intractable problem of poverty requires a complex solution. | The patient suffered from an intractable headache. | The stain on the carpet proved completely intractable.'),
  (197, 'intrinsic', 'belonging naturally to something', 'The stone''s intrinsic value mattered less than its historical meaning. | The intrinsic beauty of the wood shines through the polish. | Freedom is considered an intrinsic human right. | The intrinsic flaws in the system finally caused it to crash. | The intrinsic motivation to learn is crucial for students.'),
  (198, 'inveterate', 'deep rooted and habitual', 'Because she was an inveterate reader she carried a novel everywhere. | The inveterate gambler lost his house at the casino. | He is an inveterate liar who cannot be trusted. | The inveterate traveler has visited over a hundred countries. | My grandfather is an inveterate tinkerer who fixes everything.'),
  (199, 'irascible', 'easily moved to anger', 'The irascible coach shouted even when players followed instructions. | The irascible old man yelled at the children on his lawn. | Her irascible temper made her difficult to live with. | The irascible chef threw a pan across the kitchen. | Hunger makes him completely irascible.'),
  (200, 'judicious', 'showing wise judgment', 'A judicious editor preserves a writer''s voice while removing confusion. | The mayor made a judicious decision regarding the city budget. | The recipe calls for a judicious amount of spice. | The general made a judicious tactical retreat. | The judicious use of resources saved the company.'),
  (201, 'laconic', 'using few words', 'His laconic reply revealed neither excitement nor disappointment. | The laconic cowboy simply nodded his head. | The laconic text message read simply ''No.'' | The general is famous for his laconic battle commands. | A laconic style of writing can be very powerful.'),
  (202, 'languid', 'slow and weak', 'The speech had a languid pace that made the audience restless. | The heat made us all feel languid and lazy. | The river flowed in a languid stream toward the sea. | She raised a languid hand in greeting. | The patient showed a languid response to the medication.'),
  (203, 'laudable', 'deserving of praise', 'The group''s laudable goal was weakened by poor planning. | The charity''s laudable efforts saved hundreds of animals. | His laudable honesty in returning the wallet impressed the police. | The initiative is highly laudable even if it fails. | Her laudable dedication to the project was rewarded.'),
  (204, 'lethargic', 'sluggish and tired', 'The class seemed lethargic after the long assembly. | The heavy meal made him feel lethargic all afternoon. | The lethargic lizard basked slowly in the sun. | The economy remains lethargic despite the tax cuts. | The medication''s side effects include a lethargic feeling.'),
  (205, 'lucid', 'clear and easy to understand', 'The witness gave a lucid account of events despite the stress. | The author presents a lucid explanation of quantum physics. | The fever broke and the patient became lucid again. | The instructions must be lucid enough for a child to follow. | She had a lucid dream where she controlled her actions.'),
  (206, 'magnanimous', 'generous toward rivals', 'The winner was magnanimous and praised the skill of her opponent. | The king was magnanimous in victory and spared the rebel leaders. | It was a magnanimous gesture to forgive the debt. | The magnanimous donor funded the entire hospital wing. | The magnanimous spirit of the community helped rebuild the town.'),
  (207, 'malleable', 'easily shaped or influenced', 'The metal is malleable when heated and brittle when cold. | A child''s mind is highly malleable and absorbs everything. | The clay remains malleable as long as it is kept wet. | The malleable politician changed his views based on the polls. | Gold is an incredibly malleable element.'),
  (208, 'meticulous', 'extremely careful with details', 'The meticulous archivist recorded every change to the fragile documents. | The meticulous tailor ensured every seam was perfect. | She kept a meticulous record of all her expenses. | The meticulous planning prevented any disasters during the launch. | He is meticulous about keeping his workspace organized.'),
  (209, 'mitigate', 'make less severe', 'Trees can mitigate urban heat by shading streets and buildings. | The government took steps to mitigate the effects of the drought. | The lawyer tried to mitigate her client''s sentence. | A good shock absorber will mitigate the bumps in the road. | They implemented software to mitigate the cyber threat.'),
  (210, 'modicum', 'a small amount', 'The plan had a modicum of merit despite its unrealistic timeline. | The judge expects at least a modicum of respect in his courtroom. | The new evidence provided a modicum of hope for the defense. | A modicum of effort would have saved the project. | He showed not even a modicum of remorse for his crime.'),
  (211, 'morose', 'gloomy and sullen', 'After the defeat the normally cheerful captain became morose. | The morose teenager locked himself in his room. | A morose silence fell over the dinner table. | The movie''s morose tone depressed the audience. | The rainy weather made everyone feel morose.'),
  (212, 'mundane', 'ordinary and unremarkable', 'The essay finds beauty in mundane tasks such as washing dishes. | The mundane reality of office work crushed his artistic dreams. | They discussed mundane topics like the weather and traffic. | The mundane design of the building hides its advanced technology. | She escaped her mundane life through reading fantasy novels.'),
  (213, 'nascent', 'just beginning to develop', 'The nascent industry grew quickly once investors recognized its potential. | The nascent democracy faces many challenges. | The scientist observed the nascent stages of cell division. | The nascent political movement gained popularity online. | A nascent talent for music was obvious in the young child.'),
  (214, 'nebulous', 'vague and unclear', 'The proposal remained nebulous until the team identified specific goals. | The concept of fairness can sometimes seem nebulous. | The nebulous shapes in the fog terrified the travelers. | The politician gave a nebulous answer to the direct question. | The boundaries between the two departments are highly nebulous.'),
  (215, 'nuanced', 'showing subtle differences', 'Her nuanced reading avoids treating the characters as simply good or evil. | The actor gave a wonderfully nuanced performance. | The complex issue requires a nuanced debate. | The nuanced flavors of the wine developed over time. | A nuanced understanding of history is crucial.'),
  (216, 'obdurate', 'stubbornly refusing to change', 'The obdurate official refused to reconsider even after experts objected. | The obdurate mule refused to cross the bridge. | His obdurate denial of the facts frustrated everyone. | The obdurate fortress withstood months of siege. | The president remained obdurate in his decision to veto the bill.'),
  (217, 'oblique', 'indirect or slanted', 'The novelist used oblique references rather than naming the scandal directly. | He gave an oblique glance toward the door. | The politician''s oblique answers annoyed the journalists. | The sunlight struck the mirror at an oblique angle. | The oblique strategy caught the enemy completely off guard.'),
  (218, 'obscure', 'make hard to understand', 'Technical jargon can obscure ideas that are actually simple. | The thick fog obscured the lighthouse from view. | The details of the treaty obscure its true intent. | Time has obscured the origins of this ancient holiday. | The complex syntax obscures the poem''s meaning.'),
  (219, 'obtuse', 'slow to understand', 'His obtuse response showed that he missed the joke entirely. | The obtuse student failed to grasp the simple concept. | She wondered if he was being deliberately obtuse. | The obtuse angle is greater than ninety degrees. | The obtuse handling of the crisis cost the mayor the election.'),
  (220, 'opaque', 'hard to understand', 'The agency''s opaque rules left applicants unsure how decisions were made. | The opaque glass in the bathroom provided privacy. | The opaque jargon in the manual makes it useless. | The murky water was completely opaque. | The opaque financial report hid the company''s true losses.'),
  (221, 'opportunistic', 'taking selfish advantage', 'The opportunistic firm raised prices during the shortage. | The opportunistic infection struck while his immune system was weak. | The opportunistic thief grabbed the purse when she looked away. | The opportunistic politician changed parties to win the election. | The opportunistic predator waits for the weak to fall behind.'),
  (222, 'orthodox', 'conventional or accepted', 'Her findings challenged the orthodox view of how the empire declined. | The orthodox treatment for the disease is a course of antibiotics. | He holds extremely orthodox religious views. | The orthodox approach to teaching math emphasizes memorization. | The artist rejected orthodox techniques in favor of abstraction.'),
  (223, 'ostensible', 'stated but possibly false', 'The trip''s ostensible purpose was research but it also repaired alliances. | The ostensible reason for his resignation was health problems. | The ostensible leader of the group actually has very little power. | The ostensible charity was actually a front for tax evasion. | The ostensible plot hides a deeper psychological theme.'),
  (224, 'paradoxical', 'seemingly self contradictory', 'It seemed paradoxical that stricter rules produced greater creativity. | The paradoxical nature of light allows it to be both wave and particle. | The medicine had a paradoxical effect and made her worse. | The paradoxical statement revealed a hidden truth. | His paradoxical behavior confuses his friends constantly.'),
  (225, 'parochial', 'narrow in viewpoint', 'The committee''s parochial concerns ignored the region''s larger needs. | The parochial school only teaches local history. | His parochial attitude prevents him from understanding global issues. | The voters rejected the parochial candidate. | The newspaper has a highly parochial perspective.'),
  (226, 'parsimonious', 'unwilling to spend', 'The parsimonious owner delayed repairs until the roof collapsed. | The parsimonious boss refused to buy new chairs for the office. | A parsimonious approach to funding will doom the project. | The parsimonious billionaire drove a twenty-year-old car. | The government was parsimonious with its relief funds.'),
  (227, 'partisan', 'strongly biased toward a side', 'The report sounded partisan because it ignored evidence from opponents. | The partisan divide in the country has never been wider. | The newspaper''s partisan coverage favors the ruling party. | The partisan crowd booed the visiting team mercilessly. | A partisan block prevented the legislation from passing.'),
  (228, 'pedantic', 'overly focused on details', 'His pedantic corrections distracted from the speaker''s main point. | The pedantic professor insists on exact citations. | The review was overly pedantic and missed the beauty of the art. | Her pedantic nature makes her a great proofreader but a difficult friend. | The pedantic argument over grammar ruined the debate.'),
  (229, 'perfidious', 'treacherous and deceitful', 'The spy''s perfidious actions endangered colleagues who trusted him. | The perfidious ally signed a secret treaty with the enemy. | A perfidious lover breaks hearts without a second thought. | The perfidious plot to overthrow the king was discovered. | His perfidious betrayal shocked the entire team.'),
  (230, 'pervasive', 'spread throughout many places', 'A pervasive sense of uncertainty shaped every decision that year. | The pervasive smell of smoke lingered for days. | The pervasive influence of social media affects everyone. | The pervasive corruption in the city ruined the economy. | A pervasive fear gripped the town after the attack.'),
  (231, 'placate', 'calm someone''s anger', 'The airline offered refunds to placate passengers after the cancellation. | He bought her a diamond necklace to placate her. | The manager tried to placate the furious customer with a discount. | Nothing could placate the angry mob. | The treaty was designed to placate the hostile neighbor.'),
  (232, 'plausible', 'seeming reasonable or likely', 'Her explanation was plausible but still required evidence. | The movie features a highly plausible sci-fi scenario. | The suspect offered a plausible alibi for the night of the crime. | It is plausible that the climate will change drastically. | The theory is plausible but remains unproven.'),
  (233, 'polemical', 'strongly argumentative in tone', 'The book is polemical rather than balanced because it attacks every rival theory. | The journalist wrote a polemical article condemning the new law. | The polemical speech divided the audience. | A polemical tone is not appropriate for an objective encyclopedia. | The controversial film sparked a polemical debate.'),
  (234, 'pragmatic', 'practical and realistic', 'The mayor offered a pragmatic solution that both sides could accept. | A pragmatic approach focuses on results over ideology. | The pragmatic engineer chose the strongest material not the prettiest. | The pragmatic decision was to sell the failing business. | She is highly pragmatic when it comes to money.'),
  (235, 'precarious', 'dangerously unstable or uncertain', 'The ladder looked precarious after years of neglect. | The financial state of the company is highly precarious. | The climber balanced on a precarious ledge. | The peace treaty established a precarious cease-fire. | The precarious stack of books finally fell over.'),
  (236, 'precipitate', 'cause to happen suddenly', 'One careless remark can precipitate a conflict that lasts for years. | The assassination precipitated the start of the war. | The sudden drop in temperature precipitated a severe snowstorm. | The scandal precipitated his resignation from the board. | The chemical reaction precipitates a solid compound.'),
  (237, 'precipitous', 'steep or sudden', 'The company faced a precipitous decline after the product recall. | The mountain path has a precipitous drop on one side. | The precipitous rise in inflation panicked the markets. | His precipitous decision to quit shocked his family. | The cliff face was completely precipitous.'),
  (238, 'prescient', 'seeing future trends', 'The novelist was prescient about how technology would reshape friendship. | The prescient investor bought the stock before it skyrocketed. | The prescient warning saved many lives. | The science fiction movie seems incredibly prescient today. | Her prescient dreams often come true.'),
  (239, 'presumptuous', 'overly bold or forward', 'It was presumptuous to revise the plan without consulting the team. | The presumptuous guest started eating before the host sat down. | It is presumptuous to assume you know what she is thinking. | The presumptuous young intern told the CEO how to run the company. | I hope it is not presumptuous to offer my advice.'),
  (240, 'pretentious', 'trying to seem important', 'The restaurant''s pretentious menu made simple dishes sound mysterious. | The pretentious art critic used words nobody understood. | He drove a pretentious sports car to impress his neighbors. | The pretentious novel tries too hard to be philosophical. | The pretentious decor makes the house feel like a museum.'),
  (241, 'prolific', 'producing much work', 'The prolific composer wrote music for film as well as concert halls and theater. | The prolific author publishes three novels a year. | The prolific apple tree produces hundreds of pounds of fruit. | The prolific inventor held over a thousand patents. | The prolific artist filled the gallery with new paintings.'),
  (242, 'propitious', 'favorable for success', 'Clear skies made the morning propitious for launching the balloon. | The propitious alignment of events led to his promotion. | It is a propitious time to start a new business. | The propitious omen gave the soldiers courage. | The peace talks began under propitious circumstances.'),
  (243, 'prosaic', 'plain and ordinary', 'The poem turns prosaic details into moments of reflection. | The prosaic reality of farming involves a lot of hard manual labor. | The prosaic architecture of the office building is uninspiring. | He gave a prosaic description of his trip to the grocery store. | The prosaic language in the manual is exactly what is needed.'),
  (244, 'prudent', 'careful and wise', 'Saving extra supplies was prudent given the storm forecast. | It is prudent to check your mirrors before changing lanes. | The prudent investor diversified her portfolio. | A prudent approach minimizes the risk of failure. | The prudent king saved grain during the years of plenty.'),
  (245, 'quixotic', 'idealistic but impractical', 'His quixotic plan inspired volunteers but ignored basic costs. | The quixotic quest to find the lost city bankrupt the explorer. | Her quixotic desire to solve world hunger is noble but difficult. | The quixotic knight charged at the windmill. | The startup failed due to its quixotic business model.'),
  (246, 'rancorous', 'full of bitter resentment', 'The debate became rancorous after both candidates questioned each other''s motives. | The rancorous divorce destroyed the family. | The rancorous dispute over property lines lasted for years. | A rancorous mood settled over the divided meeting. | The rancorous online comments forced the website to close the forum.'),
  (247, 'recalcitrant', 'stubbornly refusing to cooperate', 'The recalcitrant printer jammed whenever the office needed urgent copies. | The recalcitrant child refused to eat his vegetables. | The recalcitrant prisoner spent time in solitary confinement. | The recalcitrant union leaders rejected every offer. | The recalcitrant horse refused to jump the fence.'),
  (248, 'recondite', 'difficult and obscure', 'The lecture on recondite physics lost most of the audience. | The book explores recondite philosophical concepts. | The recondite manuscript is written in a dead language. | He possesses recondite knowledge of medieval alchemy. | The recondite details of the tax code baffle most people.'),
  (249, 'redress', 'remedy or compensation', 'The law offered redress to workers who had been unfairly dismissed. | The victims sought redress in civil court. | The king promised redress for the wrongs committed by his soldiers. | The apology was a small step toward redress. | The union demanded immediate redress for the safety violations.'),
  (250, 'refute', 'prove to be false', 'The experiment refuted the claim that the material was waterproof. | The lawyer used phone records to refute the suspect''s alibi. | Galileo''s telescope helped refute the earth-centric model. | He wrote an essay to refute his critics. | The evidence decisively refutes the conspiracy theory.'),
  (251, 'relegate', 'assign lower status', 'The update relegated older features to a hidden menu. | The injured star player was relegated to the bench. | The failing team was relegated to a lower division. | The task of cleaning was relegated to the newest employee. | The outdated computer was relegated to the storage closet.'),
  (252, 'reproach', 'express criticism or disapproval', 'Her silence felt like a reproach after his careless comment. | His conduct was entirely beyond reproach. | The teacher''s mild reproach corrected the student''s behavior. | The look of reproach in the dog''s eyes made me feel guilty. | The official faced severe reproach for his mishandling of the crisis.'),
  (253, 'resilient', 'able to recover', 'The resilient ecosystem began to recover once pollution declined. | The resilient rubber bounces back to its original shape. | Children are often incredibly resilient after a tragedy. | The resilient athlete recovered quickly from the injury. | The economy proved resilient despite the global recession.'),
  (254, 'reticent', 'reserved or reluctant', 'The researcher was reticent about results that had not been replicated. | The normally talkative girl was strangely reticent. | He is reticent to discuss his personal life with the press. | The reticent suspect refused to answer the detective''s questions. | Her reticent nature makes her seem mysterious.'),
  (255, 'robust', 'strong and effective', 'The model remained robust even when tested with new data. | The robust coffee woke him up immediately. | The economy is robust enough to survive the crisis. | The robust defense prevented any goals. | The robust machinery is designed for heavy industrial use.'),
  (256, 'sagacious', 'wise and perceptive', 'The sagacious adviser anticipated problems before they became public. | The sagacious judge ruled with perfect fairness. | The sagacious old owl is a symbol of wisdom. | A sagacious investor buys when others are fearful. | The sagacious leader guided his people through the famine.'),
  (257, 'salient', 'most important or noticeable', 'The salient point is that the study measured behavior rather than intentions. | The salient feature of the landscape is the massive volcano. | The lawyer summarized the salient facts of the case for the jury. | The salient difference between the two models is the price. | Her salient weakness is her terrible time management.'),
  (258, 'sanguine', 'optimistic and confident', 'Economists were sanguine about recovery after several months of growth. | He remained sanguine despite the terrible news. | Her sanguine disposition makes her a joy to be around. | The team is sanguine about their chances of winning the championship. | A sanguine attitude is helpful in stressful situations.'),
  (259, 'sardonic', 'mocking and bitter', 'His sardonic remark turned applause into uneasy laughter. | The sardonic comedian targets the hypocrisy of politicians. | She gave a sardonic smile when the naive plan was proposed. | His sardonic tone makes it hard to know if he''s joking. | The novel is a sardonic critique of modern society.'),
  (260, 'scrupulous', 'careful and principled', 'The scrupulous journalist verified every quotation before publication. | The scrupulous accountant found the missing penny. | He is scrupulous in his adherence to the law. | The scrupulous cleaning left the kitchen spotless. | A scrupulous attention to detail is required for this job.'),
  (261, 'scrutinize', 'examine very closely', 'Auditors will scrutinize the accounts before approving the grant. | The detective scrutinized the crime scene for clues. | The committee scrutinized the proposal line by line. | The scientist scrutinized the data for any anomalies. | The media heavily scrutinized the candidate''s past.'),
  (262, 'solicitous', 'showing attentive concern', 'The nurse was solicitous without making the patient feel helpless. | The solicitous host ensured everyone had enough to eat. | He made solicitous inquiries about her sick mother. | The overly solicitous waiter interrupted our meal constantly. | Her solicitous nature makes her a wonderful caregiver.'),
  (263, 'soporific', 'tending to cause sleepiness', 'The lecture''s soporific pace made even interested students struggle. | The warm milk had a pleasant soporific effect. | The soporific heat of the afternoon made everyone nap. | The soporific documentary put half the audience to sleep. | The medicine is highly soporific and shouldn''t be taken before driving.'),
  (264, 'spurious', 'false or not genuine', 'The researcher removed spurious correlations that resulted from bad coding. | The spurious rumor ruined her reputation. | The painting was revealed to be a spurious copy. | The politician made a spurious claim about his opponent. | The logic of the argument is entirely spurious.'),
  (265, 'steadfast', 'firm and loyal', 'Her steadfast support kept the project alive during repeated setbacks. | The steadfast soldier refused to abandon his post. | A steadfast belief in justice drives her work. | The steadfast dog waited for its master every day. | Their steadfast friendship survived many arguments.'),
  (266, 'stringent', 'strict and demanding', 'Stringent safety standards reduced accidents in the factory. | The stringent requirements eliminated most of the applicants. | The stringent laws prevent any unauthorized access. | The stringent diet allows no sugar or carbohydrates. | The military academy is known for its stringent discipline.'),
  (267, 'succinct', 'brief and clear', 'The best summary was succinct yet captured the argument''s nuance. | Please keep your answers succinct and to the point. | The succinct instructions are easy to follow. | A succinct text message informed him of the change. | The succinct presentation won over the busy executives.'),
  (268, 'superfluous', 'more than necessary', 'The final paragraph was superfluous because it repeated earlier points. | The superfluous decorations made the room look cluttered. | A superfluous amount of salt ruined the soup. | The manual contains superfluous information that confuses users. | The superfluous buttons on the remote control are never used.'),
  (269, 'supplant', 'replace or displace', 'Digital archives began to supplant paper records in many libraries. | The new technology will eventually supplant the old systems. | The rebel leader sought to supplant the current dictator. | The invasive weed has supplanted the native flowers. | Electric cars are slowly supplanting gas-powered vehicles.'),
  (270, 'tacit', 'understood without words', 'Their tacit agreement allowed the meeting to proceed smoothly. | The tacit approval of the manager was all he needed. | There is a tacit understanding between the two rivals. | The nod was a tacit signal to begin the operation. | A tacit rule prevents employees from dating each other.'),
  (271, 'taciturn', 'habitually saying very little', 'The taciturn scientist preferred data to long explanations. | The taciturn teenager barely spoke at dinner. | The taciturn cowboy is a classic movie trope. | The taciturn driver ignored the passengers'' questions. | Being taciturn doesn''t mean you have nothing to say.'),
  (272, 'tenuous', 'weak or uncertain', 'The connection between the two events remained tenuous. | The tenuous thread snapped under the weight. | The government''s grip on power is incredibly tenuous. | The tenuous evidence was thrown out by the judge. | The tenuous peace agreement collapsed after a week.'),
  (273, 'timorous', 'fearful and hesitant', 'The timorous intern avoided presenting ideas until encouraged by her mentor. | The timorous rabbit hid in the bushes. | The timorous child clung to his mother''s hand. | A timorous knock on the door startled me. | The timorous request for a raise was denied.'),
  (274, 'tractable', 'easy to manage', 'The problem became tractable once the data were grouped by region. | The tractable dog learned the new tricks easily. | The tractable clay is perfect for beginners. | The tractable workforce accepted the new conditions. | The equation is highly tractable using this method.'),
  (275, 'transient', 'lasting only briefly', 'The storm caused transient flooding that disappeared by morning. | The transient beauty of the rainbow faded quickly. | The transient workers moved from town to town. | The transient pain from the injection was minor. | The transient nature of life is a common theme.'),
  (276, 'trenchant', 'sharp and effective', 'Her trenchant critique exposed assumptions the committee had overlooked. | The trenchant political cartoon summarized the entire scandal. | His trenchant observations made the debate lively. | The trenchant sword cut clean through the armor. | The trenchant wit of the author offended some readers.'),
  (277, 'ubiquitous', 'present almost everywhere', 'Smartphones became ubiquitous faster than many educators expected. | The ubiquitous security cameras track everything in the city. | The ubiquitous pop song played in every store. | Coffee shops are ubiquitous in the downtown area. | The ubiquitous pigeon can be found in most cities.'),
  (278, 'unequivocal', 'clear and unambiguous', 'The results provided unequivocal evidence that the treatment worked. | The general gave an unequivocal order to attack. | The answer is an unequivocal no. | The unequivocal success of the launch pleased the engineers. | The unequivocal support of the crowd energized the team.'),
  (279, 'untenable', 'impossible to defend', 'The explanation became untenable once the records were released. | The commander realized his position was untenable and retreated. | The untenable cost of the project forced its cancellation. | The untenable living conditions caused a mass migration. | His untenable argument fell apart quickly.'),
  (280, 'vacillate', 'waver between choices', 'The council continued to vacillate while costs increased. | The politician vacillated on the important issue. | The vacillating needle of the compass couldn''t find north. | He vacillated between ordering pizza and tacos. | The market is vacillating due to uncertainty.'),
  (281, 'venerable', 'respected due to age', 'The venerable institution adapted without abandoning its traditions. | The venerable old oak tree stood in the center of town. | The venerable professor retired after forty years of teaching. | The venerable clock still keeps perfect time. | The venerable monk imparted wisdom to the novices.'),
  (282, 'veracity', 'truthfulness or accuracy', 'The diary''s veracity was questioned because key dates were wrong. | The veracity of the witness''s claim is crucial to the case. | The scientific community debates the veracity of the new study. | The veracity of the ancient map was confirmed. | We must verify the veracity of the source.'),
  (283, 'verbose', 'using too many words', 'The verbose memo hid a simple request in five paragraphs. | The verbose speaker lost the attention of the audience. | The verbose manual could be shortened to one page. | The verbose politician never answers a question directly. | The verbose code is hard to read and debug.'),
  (284, 'viable', 'able to succeed', 'The plan became viable only after transportation costs fell. | The viable alternative is to build a new factory entirely. | The viable seed grew into a healthy plant. | The viable business model attracted many investors. | The viable embryo was successfully implanted.'),
  (285, 'vindicate', 'show to be right', 'The later discovery vindicated the scientist''s unpopular hypothesis. | The security footage will completely vindicate the wrongly accused man. | The vindictive critic was unhappy when the film was vindicated by the public. | History will vindicate our difficult decision. | The test results vindicate the safety of the bridge.'),
  (286, 'vitriolic', 'bitterly harsh in tone', 'The vitriolic comments discouraged reasonable discussion. | The vitriolic editorial attacked the mayor personally. | The vitriolic debate destroyed their friendship. | The vitriolic acid burned through the metal table. | The vitriolic review caused the play to close early.'),
  (287, 'volatile', 'quickly changing or unstable', 'Prices remained volatile after the supply chain failed. | The volatile chemical must be stored at a low temperature. | The volatile political situation could lead to war. | His volatile temper makes him dangerous to work with. | The volatile stock market crashed on Tuesday.'),
  (288, 'wary', 'careful and suspicious', 'Investors were wary of promises unsupported by data. | The wary cat watched the dog from the fence. | I am wary of strangers asking for money. | The wary soldiers advanced slowly through the jungle. | The wary consumer always reads the reviews first.'),
  (289, 'zealous', 'fervently committed to a cause', 'The zealous volunteer recruited classmates for every weekend cleanup. | The zealous detective worked through the night on the case. | The zealous fans painted their faces with the team colors. | The zealous preacher condemned modern conveniences. | The zealous advocate fought tirelessly for the victims.'),
  (290, 'auspicious', 'favorable for future success', 'The first trial produced auspicious results for a project many had doubted. | The sunny morning was an auspicious start to the journey. | The auspicious omen was interpreted as a sign of victory. | The auspicious beginning of the novel hooks the reader instantly. | The auspicious meeting secured the massive contract.'),
  (291, 'compunction', 'uneasy feeling of guilt', 'She copied the paragraph without compunction until the teacher discussed plagiarism. | The ruthless businessman fired the workers without a shred of compunction. | The thief showed no compunction during the trial. | A twinge of compunction made him return the found wallet. | The compunction hit him hard when he saw her crying.'),
  (292, 'decry', 'criticize publicly and strongly', 'Activists decried the policy because it punished families with few alternatives. | The mayor decried the violence in the streets. | The citizens decried the enormous tax increase. | The scientists decried the lack of funding for research. | The author decried the censorship of his latest book.'),
  (293, 'evince', 'show clearly or reveal', 'The careful footnotes evince the author''s respect for skeptical readers. | The child''s tears evince a deep sense of loss. | The architectural ruins evince a highly advanced ancient culture. | His letters evince a growing sense of paranoia. | The glowing review evinces the film''s broad appeal.'),
  (294, 'impede', 'slow or block', 'Fallen trees impeded the rescue crews after the storm. | The heavy traffic will impede our progress. | The cumbersome regulations impede economic growth. | The injury impeded her ability to play tennis. | The lack of clear communication impeded the project.'),
  (295, 'latent', 'present but hidden', 'The crisis revealed a latent weakness in the city''s emergency plan. | The latent virus flared up when his immune system dropped. | The latent fingerprint was discovered on the glass. | The latent talent of the quiet student was finally noticed. | The latent heat in the ocean fuels the hurricane.'),
  (296, 'levity', 'inappropriate lack of seriousness', 'The lawyer''s levity seemed disrespectful during such a serious hearing. | A little levity is needed to break the tension in the room. | The funeral was surprisingly filled with moments of levity. | His constant levity makes it hard to discuss serious matters. | The levity of the cartoon contrasts with its dark themes.'),
  (297, 'mollify', 'soothe someone''s anger', 'The principal tried to mollify parents by explaining the revised schedule. | The airline offered free tickets to mollify the stranded passengers. | The baby was finally mollified by a warm bottle. | The politician attempted to mollify his critics with a compromise. | Nothing he said could mollify her fury.'),
  (298, 'opprobrium', 'harsh public criticism', 'The official faced opprobrium after the hidden payments became public. | The scandal brought widespread opprobrium to the entire team. | The dictator ignored the international opprobrium regarding his actions. | The opprobrium of his peers forced him to resign. | The controversial article earned the magazine severe opprobrium.'),
  (299, 'rarefied', 'exclusive and specialized', 'The conference brought together a rarefied group of experts in ancient scripts. | The climber struggled to breathe in the rarefied air near the summit. | The rarefied atmosphere of the elite club intimidated him. | The rarefied concepts are only understood by a few mathematicians. | The rarefied world of high fashion is notoriously cutthroat.'),
  (300, 'rescind', 'cancel in an official way', 'The university voted to rescind the rule after students challenged it. | The company decided to rescind the controversial job offer. | The government rescinded the mandatory curfew. | The order to attack was rescinded at the last moment. | The invitation was rescinded following his terrible behavior.')
